WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.780
They say that the first impression&nbsp;
is the last impression — but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.960
if everything after that first&nbsp;
impression sucks? These infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.880
flicks piqued viewers' interests… only&nbsp;
to drop the ball mere moments later.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.480
The third "Austin Powers" installment starts off&nbsp;
with such a bang that it's a shame the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.160
movie can't live up to it. The opening set piece&nbsp;
is an over-the-top action parody of Austin himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.420
with none other than Tom Cruise donning the&nbsp;
shag-a-licious secret agent's thick glasses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.240
'60s threads. Kevin Spacey shows up as Dr. Evil,&nbsp;
Danny DeVito plays Mini-Me, and Gwyneth Paltrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.740
stars as Austin's love interest. It is revealed to&nbsp;
be directed by none other than Steven Spielberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.580
who responds to Austin's criticisms of the&nbsp;
sequence by holding up one of his three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.900
Oscars. It all ends with an elaborate dance&nbsp;
number over the opening credits, featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.880
further cameos by Quincy Jones and Britney Spears&nbsp;
as a fembot. It's a testament to the franchise's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.839
popularity at the time that so many A-listers&nbsp;
volunteered their talents for just a few minutes.

00:00:52.839 --> 00:00:55.600
"Very shagadelic, baby! Yeah!”

00:00:56.267 --> 00:01:00.300
A weird amalgamation of "Goldfinger" and&nbsp;
"Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.140
the film finds Austin Powers traveling back&nbsp;
in time to rescue his kidnapped father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.460
British secret agent Nigel Powers,&nbsp;
from Dr. Evil. When he lands in 1975,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.660
Powers teams up with American agent Foxxy&nbsp;
Cleopatra — played by none other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.640
Beyonce — to keep Dr. Evil from taking over the&nbsp;
world with Goldmember, a Dutch supervillain with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.200
peeling skin and golden genitals. Although&nbsp;
the movie has its fair share of laughs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.320
the spark from the first two is missing,&nbsp;
and the critical reaction reflects that.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.640
The success of "Who Framed Roger&nbsp;
Rabbit" inspired animation legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.360
Ralph Bakshi to try his hand at a&nbsp;
version for adults. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.280
the man who made cartoons X-rated with "Fritz the&nbsp;
Cat" had lost some of his edge by the time "Cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.360
World" came around. He seemed to have lost&nbsp;
some of his skills as a filmmaker as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.280
since the live-action and animated elements&nbsp;
rarely seem to be existing within the same frame.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.980
The film begins in 1940s Las Vegas, where WWII&nbsp;
vet Frank Harris is somehow teleported into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.540
animated universe of Cool World, eventually&nbsp;
becoming a police detective. Flash forward to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.820
1992, when animator Jack Deebs is released from&nbsp;
a 10-year prison sentence. While behind bars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.020
he created the successful comic strip "Cool&nbsp;
World," based on weird dreams he'd been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.220
having. Jack is sucked into the animated realm,&nbsp;
where he's seduced by his cartoon creation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.640
Holli Would. Holli wants to become&nbsp;
a real woman by sleeping with Jack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.720
even though sex between "doodles"&nbsp;
and "noids" is strictly forbidden.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.534
Although "Cool World" has developed&nbsp;
a bit of a cult following,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.534 --> 00:02:25.680
it was universally panned by critics when&nbsp;
it was first released. Few people found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.220
much of anything to like about it,&nbsp;
especially when held up against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.468
near-seamless integration of animation and&nbsp;
live-action in "Who Framed Roger Rabbit."

00:02:33.468 --> 00:02:37.320
Say what you will about "Fast &amp; Furious"&nbsp;
— the critically maligned fourth entry in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.640
the long-running franchise — but it features&nbsp;
a banger of an opening chase scene. A bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.800
a retcon reset after "Tokyo Drift," it begins in&nbsp;
the Dominican Republic, where Dominic Toretto is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.940
still hiding out after the events of the first&nbsp;
film caused him to flee LA. With the help of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.560
girlfriend Letty and his pal Han, Dom hijacks a&nbsp;
couple of fuel tankers by racing alongside them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.940
in his muscle car. It's a high-octane sequence&nbsp;
that shows off director Justin Lin's talent for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.960
staging the kind of elaborate action set&nbsp;
pieces that became the series' trademark.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.880
But things fall apart quickly when Dom returns&nbsp;
to Los Angeles to avenge Letty's supposed death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.020
Although it's fun to see Diesel reunite with&nbsp;
Paul Walker as former cop Brian O'Conner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.700
the duo's plot to take down a drug kingpin&nbsp;
inspires few fireworks. Admittedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.200
the "Fast &amp; Furious" series had yet to find&nbsp;
its footing by the time this film came along:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.200
It wasn't until the next installment,&nbsp;
"Fast Five," that they ditched the formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.000
and shifted the focus towards a found&nbsp;
family of globe-trotting crime fighters.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.160
You can see elements of the direction&nbsp;
the franchise would take in parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.840
this movie — for better and for worse&nbsp;
— but nothing Lin and company come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.935
up with can top the hair-raising&nbsp;
heist scene that kicks things off.

00:03:39.935 --> 00:03:44.160
The overwhelming anticipation for a&nbsp;
sequel to "The Silence of the Lambs"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.180
quickly dissipated the moment Thomas&nbsp;
Harris' novel "Hannibal" was released.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:53.520
So shocking and lurid was the book's violence&nbsp;
that director Jonathan Demme, star Jodie Foster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.200
and screenwriter Ted Tally — all of whom won&nbsp;
Oscars for "Silence of the Lambs" — declined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.280
to return for this follow-up. Only Hannibal&nbsp;
Lecter himself, fellow Oscar-winner Anthony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.420
Hopkins, agreed to appear, with Ridley&nbsp;
Scott, Julianne Moore, David Mamet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.201
and Steven Zaillian taking up directing,&nbsp;
acting, and writing duties, respectively.

00:04:10.201 --> 00:04:11.920
"Are you confused?"

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.500
This being a Ridley Scott movie, "Hannibal"&nbsp;
is nothing if not beautiful to look at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.680
even when the content makes you&nbsp;
want to look away. And indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.880
there are many moments when the sheer craft alone&nbsp;
may trick you into thinking you're watching a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.440
masterpiece. From the opening sequence, when&nbsp;
the horribly disfigured Mason Verger — played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.520
by an uncredited Gary Oldman — quizzes prison&nbsp;
guard Barney as to Hannibal's whereabouts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.980
Scott creates a feeling of dread and unease,&nbsp;
aided by John Mathieson's moody cinematography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.180
Hans Zimmer's eerie score, and Norris&nbsp;
Spencer's elaborate production design.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:47.340
As the film progresses, however, you can't help&nbsp;
but sense some of the "Silence of the Lambs" magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.740
is missing. Part of the problem is Moore's casting&nbsp;
as Clarice Starling, the FBI agent tracking Lecter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.600
down as he eats his way through Europe. As great&nbsp;
of an actress as she is, she can't erase our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.460
memories of Foster's iconic performance. Yet&nbsp;
critics saved their harshest criticisms for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:07.534
the movie's violence, which was still brutal&nbsp;
even with massive deviations from the book.

00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:12.420
While leagues above the recent "Jurassic World"&nbsp;
series, "The Lost World: Jurassic Park" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:16.860
nevertheless seen as a major comedown from its&nbsp;
predecessor. It's easy to understand why Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:22.080
Spielberg would want to make a sequel to his&nbsp;
monster 1993 hit, and individual sequences remind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.820
us of his skills both as a visual storyteller&nbsp;
and as an action filmmaker. The film's opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:31.500
is particularly strong: A vacationing British&nbsp;
family decides to dock their yacht on the remote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:36.400
island of Isla Sorna, not knowing it's home to&nbsp;
some of those genetically-engineered dinosaurs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.480
Their daughter wanders off and is greeted by&nbsp;
what appears to be a friendly Compsognathus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:44.840
but their interaction turns dangerous when an&nbsp;
entire flock of the tiny predators attacks her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.734
She survives, fortunately — but that doesn't&nbsp;
make the experience any less terrifying.

00:05:49.734 --> 00:05:54.119
Once Dr. Ian Malcolm travels to the island&nbsp;
at the behest of Dr. John Hammond, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:58.680
"The Lost World" proves that there's really no&nbsp;
good way to make a "Jurassic Park" sequel … even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:04.380
though we got four more attempts at doing just&nbsp;
that. While the dinosaurs inspired awe and wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.600
in the first film, here they're seen only as&nbsp;
monsters to run away from. Given Spielberg's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.040
directorial talent, those chase sequences&nbsp;
are thrilling. Yet as critics pointed out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:18.840
the people doing the running away are little more&nbsp;
than dino food, aside from Pete Posthlethwaite's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.340
entertaining turn as a big-game hunter who's come&nbsp;
to the island in search of exciting new prey.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:30.120
"The trick is… to get him&nbsp;
to come where we want him."

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.360
Stop-motion director Henry Selick created&nbsp;
eye-popping worlds in his animated films "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.920
Nightmare Before Christmas" and "Coraline," and&nbsp;
the same is true of "Monkeybone." Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.340
the characters he creates in this&nbsp;
live-action/animation hybrid aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:48.380
nearly as compelling as their surroundings.&nbsp;
Part of the problem is Monkeybone himself:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:52.920
As voiced by John Turturro, he's a cartoon&nbsp;
creation of animator Stu Miley — portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.760
by living legend Brendan Fraser — who finds&nbsp;
himself tormented by the claymation primate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:01.739
when a car accident puts him in a coma. While&nbsp;
trapped in the bizarro realm of his imagination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.739 --> 00:07:04.720
Stu links up with Monkeybone to&nbsp;
try and get back to his fiancee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.134
only to find out the little stinker plans to&nbsp;
use his body as a way to enter the human world.

00:07:09.339 --> 00:07:13.899
Before Stu's accident, we're treated to a&nbsp;
glimpse of "Monkeybone" as an animated series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.899 --> 00:07:18.120
which is set to make him a very rich man. Although&nbsp;
the raunchy cartoon is funny in short form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.680
we soon realize that a little bit of Monkeybone&nbsp;
goes a long way, especially when he inhabits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.760
Fraser's body. Things liven up a little&nbsp;
when Stu, in an attempt to reclaim his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.440
jumps into the body of a recently deceased organ&nbsp;
donor, played by the hilarious Chris Kattan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.180
who keeps spilling his parts all over town. If the&nbsp;
middle section were as good as the beginning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:41.520
the end, "Monkeybone" might've turned out to be&nbsp;
some kind of bonkers masterpiece. As it stands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.220
however, it's an ambitious curio, one that&nbsp;
appalled critics and scared away audiences.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:51.020
Disney's association with "The Wizard of&nbsp;
Oz" goes all the way back to the 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:54.600
when Walt wanted to turn the book into an&nbsp;
animated follow-up to "Snow White and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:58.980
Seven Dwarfs." But the estate of L. Frank Baum&nbsp;
had already sold the rights to Samuel Goldwyn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:04.080
who in turn sold them to Louis B. Mayer, and the&nbsp;
rest is history. That didn't stop Disney Studios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:07.860
from trying to make their own version of the&nbsp;
classic book series — including the surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.280
dark "Return to Oz" — culminating in the&nbsp;
critically maligned "Oz the Great and Powerful."

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:14.560
"Stop! You're hurting him."

00:08:19.693 --> 00:08:24.780
Directed by Sam Raimi, it stars James Franco as&nbsp;
Oscar Diggs, a traveling circus magician and con&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:28.740
man who's plucked out of Kansas by a tornado&nbsp;
and plopped down in the magical Land of Oz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:33.420
The locals think Oscar is a great wizard who's&nbsp;
come to save them, which he decides to use for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:37.920
his financial gain. His magical abilities come&nbsp;
under scrutiny from three witches — Theodora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.499
Evanora, and Glinda — each of whom hopes to&nbsp;
employ him in their fight for control of Oz.

00:08:42.499 --> 00:08:46.320
Copyright laws kept Raimi from using some&nbsp;
of the most iconic characters and imagery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.940
from the 1939 "Wizard of Oz," including&nbsp;
Dorothy's ruby red slippers. The beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:55.200
of his film — which follows Oscar's origins&nbsp;
in Kansas — is a callback to the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:00.240
shot in black-and-white Academy ratio. It's&nbsp;
so good it makes you wish he had the rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:05.734
to make an official prequel rather than, well,&nbsp;
whatever the heck this movie turned out to be.

00:09:05.734 --> 00:09:10.080
Tim Burton had firmly established himself as one&nbsp;
of Hollywood's most visually inventive filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.520
by the time he took a crack at remaking "Planet&nbsp;
of the Apes." Unlike the most recent trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:18.600
which employed motion capture CGI to&nbsp;
turn humans into apes, the director used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:22.320
practical makeup effects courtesy of Rick&nbsp;
Baker. And this being a Tim Burton film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.400
you know the art direction is going to be&nbsp;
similarly dazzling. If the screenplay were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:29.760
up to that same level of craft, there&nbsp;
might have really been something here.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.020
Hope springs eternal when the film opens,&nbsp;
as astronaut Leo Davidson crash lands on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:38.460
mysterious planet where humans are enslaved and&nbsp;
apes reign supreme. There's a goofy charm to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:42.120
Mark Wahlberg's performance as a spaceman&nbsp;
who goes into orbit to save his partner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.560
a chimpanzee named Pericles, only to find&nbsp;
himself captured by intellectually advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.420
primates. The ape performances by Tim Roth,&nbsp;
Helena Bonham Carter, Michael Clarke Duncan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:54.900
and Paul Giamatti are fully committed&nbsp;
despite being hidden under pounds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:59.639
makeup. And not for nothing, but there&nbsp;
are also some impressive action sequences.

00:09:59.639 --> 00:10:03.240
Yet as the film progresses,&nbsp;
you get the sinking feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.500
that the three credited screenwriters&nbsp;
— William Broyles Jr., Lawrence Konner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:12.540
and Mark Rosenthal — didn't know exactly&nbsp;
what tone to strike. Worst of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:16.560
the customary twist ending raises more&nbsp;
questions than it answers. The finished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:23.119
product was a critical disaster that failed to&nbsp;
relaunch a franchise… and featured, well… this:

00:10:30.976 --> 00:10:35.340
Roger Moore's final outing as James Bond in&nbsp;
"A View to a Kill" was a real low point for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:41.280
the series, earning some of the franchise's&nbsp;
worst reviews. Yet even the worst 007 outing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.134
still has a great opening sequence — albeit one&nbsp;
with a very noticeable stunt double for Moore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.134 --> 00:10:49.920
who at 57 was starting to show&nbsp;
his age. Bond is dispatched to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.000
Siberia to retrieve a microchip from a fallen&nbsp;
agent. When he's discovered by the bad guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.040
he has to evade them by sledding down the icy&nbsp;
mountain, first on skis, then on a snowmobile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.940
and finally on a makeshift snowboard —&nbsp;
all while accompanied by the Beach Boys'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.980
classic "California Girls." After blowing&nbsp;
up the helicopter that's been chasing him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:11.580
Bond enters a hidden submarine and gets cozy with&nbsp;
the beautiful submariner. And of course, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:16.560
the opening credits sequence, set to Duran Duran's&nbsp;
theme song, aptly titled "A View to a Kill."

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:20.940
Bond later finds out the microchip is a product&nbsp;
of Zorin Industries, led by the villainous Max&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:25.560
Zorin — played by Christopher Walken doing&nbsp;
his best bad-guy act. Zorin has some nefarious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:29.400
scheme to become the world's leading microchip&nbsp;
manufacturer by wiping out Silicon Valley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:34.980
aided by his henchwoman May Day. By this point in&nbsp;
Moore's run, the series had become so campy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:39.900
lightweight that it was difficult to take anything&nbsp;
seriously. Although die-hard fans will still stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:45.000
up for this film, it was clear that a massive&nbsp;
reset was necessary to keep the franchise alive.

